WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.935
Film Courage: What does a good opening of a&nbsp;
movie have to have? Chris Gore, Founder/Owner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.935 --> 00:00:06.320
of FilmThreat/Author/Filmmaker: It's just really&nbsp;
simple I want to know what's going to happen next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:12.560
and I can't tell you how many times I've&nbsp;
seen the first five or 10 minutes of a movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:17.360
and I don't want to watch the rest of it. For&nbsp;
a couple years I was a programmer at the Sonoma&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:25.520
Valley film festival and was a program director&nbsp;
and I watched films and you can tell within the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:30.480
first few minutes, you want to know what's&nbsp;
going to happen next. You have to be engaged,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:35.520
you have to be drawn in, it's got to be&nbsp;
something emotional. You have to connect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:46.800
and you just really have to know what's going to&nbsp;
happen next. You can have like a great opening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:51.440
and and that can actually if you don't have that&nbsp;
great opening it's not gonna propel the rest of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:56.240
the film because the middle of the movie can get&nbsp;
muddled. I think middles of films are tough but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.240 --> 00:01:02.080
if you blow the opening I mean there's no point&nbsp;
in watching the rest. I can't tell you how many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:07.920
first 10 minutes of movies I've watched. So you&nbsp;
have to grab the viewer in the first 10 minutes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:16.080
and it really comes to do I want to continue&nbsp;
on the story with this character? Film Courage:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:20.080
Can you think of one that's like the&nbsp;
perfect opening, that stands out as a model,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:24.400
that you would never feel you could live up&nbsp;
against? Chris: See my taste is so weird.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:31.360
I'm a terrible person to ask that question of&nbsp;
'what's a great opening,' but I'll say that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:36.240
probably one of my favorite independent films&nbsp;
of all time is Richard Linklater's Slacker.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:44.240
It's a totally non-traditional narrative. It&nbsp;
follows different people's stories sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:52.240
set in a day in Austin Texas. It's very much the&nbsp;
flavor of that town at that time and I love that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:57.600
movie. I don't know what it is? I think it's some&nbsp;
of the stories are more engaging than others, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:08.960
I want to live in that world. So that's a&nbsp;
terrible example because it's not a traditional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:16.880
narrative structure. It's a series of pieces&nbsp;
of stories leading you through slice of life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:24.000
truly slice of life. Film Courage: What are three&nbsp;
mistakes that most filmmakers or screenwriters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:30.640
make in the first act of a film? Chris: I&nbsp;
would say that the biggest mistake that I see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:39.200
that a filmmaker will make in the first act&nbsp;
is sort of lack of world building. To me I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:45.680
would say that this probably applies more to genre&nbsp;
pictures. World building is incredibly important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:55.680
to set up those rules, set up expectations&nbsp;
and defy those expectations and if you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.680 --> 00:03:03.040
not established that, nothing in the rest of the&nbsp;
film is going to work. So in that first act world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:08.880
building is incredibly important. Film Courage:&nbsp;
What are you looking for in a story? Chris: What I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:13.600
always look for in a story is take me on a journey&nbsp;
and make me feel something. I want to feel moved.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:22.480
I don't have to agree with everything in the&nbsp;
film but I want to be taken on a journey where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:29.040
I'll feel different out the other side. I think&nbsp;
the films that have always impressed me the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:37.040
are the ones that have just made me look at life&nbsp;
differently. There's a short list of films that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:41.520
fit that category. I would say that Spike Lee's&nbsp;
Do The Right Thing is one for me that I remember.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:46.800
When I saw that film when I was a kid it blew&nbsp;
my mind. A chain might change my perspective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:54.800
of the world it has an incredible opening scene&nbsp;
with that music blaring is like it's that title&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.800 --> 00:04:07.120
sequence is so incredible. But there's a sweetness&nbsp;
to that world and it's such an original vision.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:13.200
I don't know, I remember seeing that film&nbsp;
and just feeling different afterwards and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:21.040
that applies to other more mainstream movies&nbsp;
like a Star Wars or a Lord of the Rings. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:30.800
make me feel something, move me in some&nbsp;
way and surprise me. Make me want to go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:38.400
on this journey and then if we're luckyI have a&nbsp;
changed perspective. Film Courage: What is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:44.560
purpose of story? Chris: Wow, that is such&nbsp;
a...these are big questions you're asking.

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:53.520
I really believe that the purpose of stories&nbsp;
is for us to experience things we may never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.520 --> 00:05:03.040
experience in our lifetime as a way for us to&nbsp;
heal based on our own personal experience. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:12.960
experiencing a story that may have to do with the&nbsp;
death of a loved one may help myself deal with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:19.520
loss of a loved one in my own life. I feel like&nbsp;
there's almost films when I talk to friends and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:25.120
they'll say they're going through some you&nbsp;
know trouble whether it be work related or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:33.360
love life or whatnot or something tragic&nbsp;
that's happened I often prescribe a movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:42.000
because through the experience of a character&nbsp;
in a film we can help heal a part of ourselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:49.600
and that's what I think story is about to impart&nbsp;
those lessons in a way that's safe. You know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:57.360
I've never been to war, I've never experienced&nbsp;
what it's like to be in war. I've had relatives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.360 --> 00:06:02.960
my grandfather fought in World War II. He was&nbsp;
in the Navy. I had an uncle who was in Vietnam&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:08.640
and having never gone through that&nbsp;
experience I won't know what that's like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:15.520
but having experienced films like Saving Private&nbsp;
Ryan, Dunkirk, some of the great war films&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.480 --> 00:06:27.920
Platoon among others. Oliver Stone's Platoon,&nbsp;
those visceral war films really put you in that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:33.840
seat. Full Metal Jacket by Stanley Kubrick like so&nbsp;
that comes to mind. I'm grateful that I've never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:44.880
had that kind of experience but I do think&nbsp;
that really the purpose is to teach us things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:52.000
and to hopefully teach us something about&nbsp;
ourselves and a really good movie it's just a very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:58.880
personal experience. That's why I think people&nbsp;
get offended by bad reviews and by that I mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.640 --> 00:07:08.720
as someone who has spent a good chunk of my career&nbsp;
as a critic what's weird is the reaction of people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:15.280
